Abstract: We propose explicit constant-evaluated decorators that compose returned descriptions of one class or function definition.
Abstract:
Abstract: Applications run from the nearest outward, passing each returned description and its computed annotations to the next application before the compiler installs the final definition. Structural validators may return their input unchanged. The proposed contract permits edits to authored declarations while preserving destination identity, retained bindings, and diagnostic origin. This semantic model remains unimplemented and requires wording and compiler evidence. The examples establish no fundamental expressiveness advantage over existing generation facilities. Independent per-application retention and mandatory checks of completed classes and specializations remain unmet requirements outside this narrow core. Completion checks add an execution point and independent retention may add compiler-managed publication state. Their integration can change the execution model. We request direction on this contract for composing semantic edits while keeping those requirements explicit.
